format: 1
world: kubenix
module-attr: kubenixModules
schema-pin: LIPS_KUBENIX_FLAKE
input-args: , kubenix
claims: sandbox

--- preamble ---
TARGET WORLD: kubenix. A kubenix configuration renders KUBERNETES
MANIFESTS: nothing is installed and no machine is configured, so never emit
a NixOS or home-manager option here (no services.*, no systemd.*, no
environment.*). Its namespace is kubernetes.resources.<kindPlural>.<name>.*
-- the plural, lower-camel kind name, then the resource's own name, then the
fields of the Kubernetes API object itself
(kubernetes.resources.deployments.<self>.spec.replicas,
kubernetes.resources.services.<self>.spec.ports). <self> keys the resource
name, so one program's resources all carry its instance name and a sibling
instance composes without collision. Beside the resources, this world offers
kubernetes.namespace (the namespace resources land in) and kubenix.project
(a label put on every object). query_options searches exactly this world's
pinned schema: the kubenix option tree, whose resource fields come from the
Kubernetes API, nothing else. Write the alias path
(kubernetes.resources.*), never the kubernetes.api.resources.* spelling
behind it.

A CLAIM MUST NAME THE PROGRAM'S OWN ARTIFACT here: there is no machine to
boot in this world, so an observable is a command over ${artifact.<name>}
and literal text, run in the build sandbox. A claim reaching anything else is
refused.

--- schema ---
# kubenix declares its Kubernetes resource fields as module options generated
# from the Kubernetes API, so the document comes from nixosOptionsDoc over an
# otherwise EMPTY kubenix evaluation: the option TREE is what grounds a rule,
# and no program's own values may enter the schema. nixpkgs comes from the
# kubenix flake's OWN pinned input, never resolved ambiently: a grounding
# schema must be reproducible from the recorded ref alone.
#
# The jq pass is this world's shape, not lips': it re-keys the generated
# kubernetes.api.resources.* paths to the kubernetes.resources.* alias a
# program writes, unwraps the `null or X` optionals kubenix's generator emits,
# and drops every inner node whose own children carry the fields (a resource's
# submodule appears both as a node and as its leaves). sort_by(.key | split("."))
# is load-bearing: the adjacency test below needs SEGMENT order, not string
# order, or a name like `a.b-c` breaks it.
let k = builtins.getFlake "<flakeref>";
    system = builtins.currentSystem;
    pkgs = import k.inputs.nixpkgs { inherit system; };
    e = k.evalModules.${system} {
      module = { kubenix, ... }: { imports = [ kubenix.modules.k8s ]; };
    };
    doc = (pkgs.nixosOptionsDoc { options = e.options; warningsAreErrors = false; }).optionsJSON;
in pkgs.runCommand "options.json" { } ''
  ${pkgs.jq}/bin/jq '
    to_entries
    | map(.key |= (if startswith("kubernetes.api.resources.")
          then "kubernetes.resources." + .[25:] else . end))
    | map(.value.type |= (if (type == "string") and startswith("null or ")
          then (.[8:] | if startswith("(") and endswith(")") then .[1:-1] else . end)
          else . end))
    | sort_by(.key | split("."))
    | . as $es
    | [ range(0; length) as $i | $es[$i]
        | select(($i + 1 >= ($es | length))
            or (($es[$i+1].key | startswith($es[$i].key + ".")) | not)) ]
    | from_entries
  ' ${doc}/share/doc/nixos/options.json > $out
''

--- inputs ---
  inputs.kubenix.url = "github:hall/kubenix";

--- builds ---
      # The per-system kubenix evaluation, defined once so the buildable
      # manifest (packages) and the printing rung (apps) reference the SAME
      # rendered file. Both output spellings are kubenix's OWN options
      # (resultYAML, result), so lips converts nothing and owns no format code.
      builds = system:
        let
          cfg = (kubenix.evalModules.${system} {
            module = { kubenix, ... }: {
              imports = [ kubenix.modules.k8s ./default.nix ];
            };
          }).config.kubernetes;
        in { yaml = cfg.resultYAML; json = cfg.result; };

--- packages ---
        manifest = (builds system).yaml;
        manifest-json = (builds system).json;

--- apps ---
        let b = builds system; p = pkgsFor system; in {
          manifest = { type = "app"; program = "${p.writeShellScript "print-manifest" "cat ${b.yaml}"}"; };
          manifest-json = { type = "app"; program = "${p.writeShellScript "print-manifest-json" "cat ${b.json}"}"; };
        }

--- devShells ---
        let p = pkgsFor system; in {
          default = p.mkShell { packages = [ p.kubectl ]; };
        }

--- rungs ---
write the manifests | run | manifest | > manifests.yaml
check it renders | build | manifest |
the JSON form | run | manifest-json | > manifests.json
a shell with kubectl | develop | |
